        "correct_answer_explanation": "**Correct Answer: B - Warfarin-induced skin necrosis due to Protein C deficiency**\n\n**PATHOPHYSIOLOGY:**\n- Protein C (natural anticoagulant) has shorter half-life (6-8 hours) than vitamin K-dependent procoagulant factors (24-72 hours)\n- Warfarin inhibits ALL vitamin K-dependent factors (II, VII, IX, X + Protein C, Protein S)\n- In Protein C deficiency: Protein C levels drop FIRST (within hours) before procoagulant factors decrease\n- **Result**: Paradoxical prothrombotic state → microvascular thrombosis → skin necrosis\n- Typically occurs 3-6 days after warfarin initiation (this patient: 6 hours suggests severe deficiency)\n\n**CLINICAL FEATURES:**\n- Painful erythematous lesions → purpura → necrosis with eschar formation\n- **Classic sites**: Breasts, buttocks, thighs, penis (areas with high adipose tissue)\n- Occurs early in warfarin therapy (days 3-6, occasionally within hours)\n- **Differential**: HIT (platelet drop, later onset 5-10 days), purpura fulminans (sepsis features)\n\n**IMMEDIATE MANAGEMENT:**\n1. **Cease warfarin immediately** (prevent further Protein C suppression)\n2. **Continue LMWH** (therapeutic dose) for anticoagulation\n3. **Protein C replacement**: Fresh frozen plasma (FFP) 10-15 mL/kg OR Protein C concentrate (if available)\n4. **Avoid further warfarin** - transition to long-term LMWH or DOAC (rivaroxaban/apixaban safe)\n5. Wound care: Surgical debridement if extensive necrosis\n\n**LONG-TERM:**\n- Lifelong anticoagulation required (unprovoked bilateral DVT + Protein C deficiency)\n- **Avoid warfarin** (absolute contraindication after skin necrosis)\n- Options: LMWH (pregnancy), DOACs (rivaroxaban/apixaban preferred)\n- Screen family members (autosomal dominant, 50% risk)\n\n**WHY OTHER OPTIONS INCORRECT:**\n\n**Option A (HIT):** Skin lesions NOT typical of HIT\n- HIT: Platelet drop >50%, thrombosis (arterial/venous), later onset (5-10 days)\n- HIT skin lesions: Injection site necrosis, acral ischae